---
title: Hex reverse
tags: tools
heading: "Reverse hex"
summary: >-
    Swaps the digits of each byte, reverses the bits in each byte or nibble, or reverses the order of the bytes, which
    isn't the same as reversing the whole string.
---
{% from "macros/tool.njk" import toolSection with context %}
{% from "macros/tool-frame.njk" import toolFrame %}
{% from "macros/delimiter.njk" import delimiter %}
{% set filename = page.fileSlug %}
{% set intro %}
    <p>Reversing the whole string turns <code class="tool-example">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> [Hello] into <code class="tool-example">f6 c6 c6 56 84</code>. These reverse it in other ways:</p>
    <dl>
        <dt>Digits in each byte</dt>
        <dd>Swaps the two hex digits of each byte, which is the same as swapping its two nibbles, so <code class="tool-example">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="tool-example">84 56 c6 c6 f6</code></dd>
        <dt>Bits in each byte</dt>
        <dd>Reverses the order of all 8 bits within each byte, so <code class="tool-example">48</code> [01001000] becomes <code class="tool-example">12</code> [00010010], and <code class="tool-example">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="tool-example">12 a6 36 36 f6</code></dd>
        <dt>Bits in each nibble</dt>
        <dd>Reverses the bits within each nibble (4 bits) of every byte, leaving the nibbles in place. It's the same as converting the hex to binary, reversing the bits in each nibble, then converting it back to hex: <code class="tool-example">48</code> [0100 1000] becomes <code class="tool-example">21</code> [0010 0001], and <code class="tool-example">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="tool-example">21 6a 63 63 6f</code></dd>
        <dt>Byte order</dt>
        <dd>While it sounds similar, byte order is a little different: this reverses the order of the bytes in the string, leaving each byte as it is, so <code class="tool-example">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="tool-example">6f 6c 6c 65 48</code></dd>
    </dl>
    <p>Your hex can use any of the usual delimiters between bytes (spaces, hyphens, colons, commas, semicolons, new lines, \x, 0x or %), or none, and they can be mixed. The delimiter dropdown chooses how the result is written.</p>
{% endset %}
{% call toolSection(intro) %}
{% call toolFrame(filename, "Reverse hex", placeholder = "Example: 48 65 6c 6c 6f", actions = [{ id: filename + "Run", label: "Reverse" }]) %}
<div class="tool-field">
    <label class="tool-field__label" for="{{ filename }}Type"><span class="visually-hidden">What to </span>Reverse</label>
    <select class="form-select" id="{{ filename }}Type" autocomplete="off">
        <option value="digits" selected="">Digits in each byte</option>
        <option value="bits">Bits in each byte</option>
        <option value="nibbles">Bits in each nibble</option>
        <option value="byte-order">Byte order</option>
    </select>
</div>
<div class="tool-field">
    <label class="tool-field__label" for="{{ filename }}Delimiter"><span class="visually-hidden">Hex </span>Delimiter</label>
    {{ delimiter(filename) }}
</div>
{% endcall %}
{% endcall %}
